000f6;rster</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Safe handling of nanotechnology</article-title><source>Nature</source><volume>444</volume><issue>7117</issue><fpage>267</fpage><lpage>269</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17108940</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b7-ehp0115-001654"><citation citation-type="web"><collab>MIAME (Minimum Information About a Microarray Experiment)</collab><year>2007</year><source>Home Page</source><comment>Available: <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.mged.org/Workgroups/MIAME/miame.html">http://www.mged.org/Workgroups/MIAME/miame.html</ext-link></comment><access-date>[accessed 3 July 2007]</access-date></citation></ref><ref id="b8-ehp0115-001654"><citation citation-type="web"><collab>MIAPE (Minimum Information About a Proteomics Experiment)</collab><year>2007</year><source>Home Page</source><comment>Available: <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://psidev.sourceforge.net/miape/">http://psidev.sourceforge.net/miape/</ext-link></comment><access-date>[accessed 3 July 2007]</access-date></citation></ref><ref id="b9-ehp0115-001654"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Nel</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Xia</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Madler</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Li</surname><given-names>N</given-names></name></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Toxic potential of materials at the nanolevel</article-title><source>Science</source><volume>311</volume><issue>5761</issue><fpage>622</fpage><lpage>627</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16456071</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b10-ehp0115-001654"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Oberd&#x000f6;rster</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Maynard</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Donaldson</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><name><surname>Castranova</surname><given-names>V</given-names></name><name><surname>Fitzpatrick</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Ausman</surname><given-names>K</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2005a</year><article-title>Principles for characterizing the potential human health effects from exposure to nanomaterials: elements of a screening strategy</article-title><source>Part Fibre Toxicol</source><volume>2</volume><fpage>8</fpage></citation></ref><ref id="b11-ehp0115-001654"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Oberd&#x000f6;rster</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Oberd&#x000f6;rster</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Oberd&#x000f6;rster</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name></person-group><year>2005b</year><article-title>Nanotoxicology: an emerging discipline evolving from studies of ultrafine particles</article-title><source>Environ Health Perspect</source><volume>113</volume><fpage>823</fpage><lpage>839</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="b12-ehp0115-001654"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Penn</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Murphy</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Barker</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Henk</surname><given-names>W</given-names></name><name><surname>Penn</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>Combustion-derived ultrafine particles transport organic toxicants to target respiratory cells</article-title><source>Environ Health Perspect</source><volume>113</volume><fpage>956</fpage><lpage>963</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16079063</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b13-ehp0115-001654"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Schwarze</surname><given-names>PE</given-names></name><name><surname>Ovrevik</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Lag</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Refsnes</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Nafstad</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Hetland</surname><given-names>RB</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2006</year><article-title>Particulate matter properties and health effects: consistency of epidemiological and toxicological studies</article-title><source>Hum Exp Toxicol</source><volume>25</volume><issue>10</issue><fpage>559</fpage><lpage>579</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17165623</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b14-ehp0115-001654"><citation citation-type="web"><collab>Woodrow Wilson International Center for Scholars</collab><source>Nanotechnology Consumer Products Inventory</source><year>2007</year><comment>Available: <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="http://www.nanotechproject.org/consumerproducts">http://www.nanotechproject.org/consumerproducts</ext-link></comment><access-date>[accessed 3 July 2007]</access-date></citation></ref><ref id="b15-ehp0115-001654"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zhao</surname><given-names>X</given-names></name><name><surname>Striolo</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Cummings</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name></person-group><year>2005</year><article-title>C60 binds to and deforms nucleotides</article-title><source>Biophys J</source><volume>89</volume><issue>6</issue><fpage>3856</fpage><lpage>3862</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16183879</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="f1-ehp0115-001654" position="float"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>The parallel relationship between material design and material testing (nanotechnology and nanotoxicology).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ehp0115-001654f1"/></fig><fig id="f2-ehp0115-001654" position="float"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>Proposal for a tiered human health hazard assessment program&#x02014;a decision algorithm for deciding which tier of testing is needed but was not produced during this workshop.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ehp0115-001654f2"/></fig><fig id="f3-ehp0115-001654" position="float"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>Judged status of available tests for different parameters of dendrimers, metal oxides, and carbon nanotubes. Abbreviations: G, green; N/A, not applicable; NPs, nanoparticles; R, red; Y, yellow. (<italic>A)</italic> Physical chemical characterization; (<italic>B)</italic> ADME/ translocation; and (<italic>C</italic>) biochemistry of nanoparticles.</p><p><sup><bold><italic>a</italic></bold></sup>Particles that can be radiolabeled can be more readily assessed for these features. <sup><bold><italic>b</italic></bold></sup>Assays are available but are very slow. <bold><italic>c</italic></bold>Labeling <italic>in vivo</italic> presents a challenge</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="ehp0115-001654f3"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>